// E0431: the run bound the postgres driver and named no database.
//
// The fault is the run's configuration rather than the program's, so this
// program is correct and checks cleanly. It fails at start-up, before anything
// evaluates:
//
//   $ ply run --host tests/fixtures/db_not_configured.ply
//   E0431  this program performs `db` operations and the run named no database
//
// and succeeds under `--db postgres://user@host:5432/database`. Without
// `--host` at all it is E0424 naming `ply_host::db::query`, exactly as every
// other host operation is: nothing is bound, so the question of which database
// is never reached.
//
// **The password is named nowhere in this file, and there is no way to name it
// here.** A connection string in a `String` literal would put a credential into
// this definition's hash and into a content-addressed store designed never to
// forget — where it could not be taken back out, and where it would also
// re-run every test in its closure on the next rotation. So the connection
// string is configured beside the run — `--db`, or `PLY_DB_URL` — and the
// password comes from `PLY_DB_PASSWORD`, because an argument is readable by
// every process on the machine. This is ADR 0013 §6's sentence about a private
// key, applied to the second credential the web track acquires.
//
// The row is the other half of what W4 is for: `list_items` declares
// `{db.read[items]}` and that is the table it touches, so what this endpoint
// can reach is in its signature rather than in a comment.

import std.db (db, Answer, stmt)

pub fn list_items() -> Answer / {db.read[items]} =
  db.query[items](stmt("select sku, on_hand from items order by sku"), [])

fn main() -> Answer / {db.read[items]} = list_items()
